This version sorts the array first, so equal values are next to each other. I scan each group and get its size. During the scan, the smallest and largest group sizes are updated.

The input array is changed by `Arrays.sort`. An array with only one distinct value is also handled, because its group size becomes both the highest and lowest frequency.

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

class Solution {
    public int sumHighestAndLowestFrequency(int[] nums) {
        Arrays.sort(nums);

        int lowest = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        int highest = 0;
        int index = 0;

        while (index < nums.length) {
            int next = index;

            while (next < nums.length && nums[next] == nums[index]) {
                next++;
            }

            int count = next - index;
            lowest = Math.min(lowest, count);
            highest = Math.max(highest, count);
            index = next;
        }

        return lowest + highest;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` because of sorting. The scan takes `O(n)`. Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space apart from the sorting implementation.

I used an `unordered_map` to count how many times each number appears. While reading the map, I keep the largest and smallest frequency. The answer is their sum.

For example, with `nums = [1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3]`, the frequencies are `1, 2, 3`. The result is `3 + 1 = 4`.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <climits>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int sumHighestAndLowestFrequency(vector<int>& nums) {
        unordered_map<int, int> frequency;

        for (int value : nums) {
            frequency[value]++;
        }

        int lowest = INT_MAX;
        int highest = 0;

        for (const auto& entry : frequency) {
            lowest = min(lowest, entry.second);
            highest = max(highest, entry.second);
        }

        return lowest + highest;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)` on average. Space complexity: `O(n)` for the frequency map.

Sorting places matching numbers beside one another. I then count each run and compare its length with the current minimum and maximum.

Dry run: for `[5, 5, 2, 2, 2, 9]`, sorting gives `[2, 2, 2, 5, 5, 9]`. The run lengths are `3, 2, 1`, so the returned sum is `1 + 3 = 4`.

